== G.2 Question 1 — Indirect Rule

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[Why did indirect rule succeed in Northern Nigeria and fail in Eastern Nigeria?]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *definition, the northern structure, the eastern structure, the consequence.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  Indirect rule was the British policy of governing a colony through existing chiefs and
  institutions, under the supervision of a British officer. It succeeded in the north
  because the Sokoto Caliphate had already built a hierarchy that ran from the emir, through
  the district head, to the village head, with Alkali courts applying Sharia at every level.
  The British had only to place a Resident above the emir and let the existing machine
  collect taxes and keep order; the emir kept his court, his council and his legitimacy, so
  the system was accepted.

  It failed in the east because the Igbo village republic had no single ruler. Authority
  lay with councils of elders, age grades, title societies and the assembly, and there was
  no throne to stand behind. The British created warrant chiefs, men appointed by warrant
  rather than by custom, and their authority was resented from the beginning. The resentment
  came to a head in the Aba women's war of 1929, when the rumoured taxation of women and the
  chiefs' abuse of power produced a revolt that forced the colonial government to replace
  many warrant chiefs with clan councils.

  The lesson is that indirect rule was not a policy that could be applied uniformly: it
  worked where a chief already ruled, and it broke where none did.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* it names the policy, contrasts the two structures, gives the 1929 example and draws the lesson. Four marks in about a hundred and eighty words.]

