== G.3 Question 2 — Why Soldiers Intervene

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[Give three reasons for military intervention in Nigerian politics, with one example of each.]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *three causes, each with a dated Nigerian example, then a one-line judgement.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  The first reason was corruption and the failure of civilian leaders. The First Republic's
  ministers were accused of using office for private gain, and the January 1966 plotters
  justified themselves in exactly those words; the same accusation returned in 1983, when
  the Second Republic was overthrown amid charges of mismanagement.

  The second was electoral malpractice. The federal election of 1964 and the Western Region
  election of 1965 were both followed by violence, and the army presented itself as the only
  institution able to restore order. In 1983 the same pattern repeated, with a disputed
  election preceding the December coup.

  The third was the weakness of civilian institutions. A parliament that could not control
  the executive, a judiciary that could not punish the powerful and a police force that
  could not keep order left the army as the only organised alternative. The ouster clause
  and the special tribunal then made sure that, once in power, the army could not be
  corrected by the courts.

  The three causes reinforce one another: corruption discredited the politicians, rigging
  removed the peaceful route to replace them, and weak institutions left the barracks as
  the only exit.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* three named causes, three dated examples, and a closing sentence that connects them. Do not write a narrative of every coup; the question asks for reasons, not history.]

