== G.4 Question 3 — The Three Lists

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[Explain the three legislative lists and state the rule that applies when federal and state laws conflict.]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *each list with two examples, then section 4(5) and the name of the doctrine.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  The exclusive list contains the subjects on which only the National Assembly may
  legislate — defence, currency, foreign affairs, citizenship and the Nigeria Police Force.
  A state law on an exclusive matter is void, because the Constitution has reserved the
  subject to the federation.

  The concurrent list contains the subjects on which both the National Assembly and a state
  house of assembly may legislate — education, health, agriculture, electricity and
  industrial development. Both may act, but they do not act as equals when they disagree.

  The residual list contains everything not named in the other two, and it belongs to the
  states: chieftaincy, local government, land in the state, markets and state roads. The
  states are the sole legislators here, which is what gives the residual list its federal
  importance.

  Where a state law is inconsistent with a federal law on a concurrent matter, section 4(5)
  of the 1999 Constitution provides that the federal law prevails and the state law is void
  to the extent of the inconsistency. The doctrine is called covering the field, and the
  qualification — to the extent of the inconsistency — is the part candidates forget.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* it gives the three lists in order, supplies examples, states the section, names the doctrine and adds the qualification. The qualification is often the difference between full and half marks.]

