== G.5 Question 4 — Afrocentrism

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[What is meant by the statement that Africa is the centre-piece of Nigeria's foreign policy?]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *define, give the circles, give three African commitments, then one limit.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  The statement means that Africa comes first in Nigeria's ranking of foreign-policy
  priorities — that African issues are treated as Nigeria's own interest, and that Nigeria
  prefers to act through African organisations. It is the doctrine called Afrocentrism.

  The doctrine is expressed in the concentric circles. The national interest is at the
  centre, West Africa is the second circle through ECOWAS, Africa is the third through the
  African Union and its predecessor the OAU, and the wider world is the outermost circle
  through the United Nations, the Commonwealth and OPEC.

  Three commitments show the doctrine in practice. Nigeria campaigned for the decolonisation
  of the continent and against apartheid in South Africa. It was a founder of ECOWAS in 1975
  and of the OAU in 1963, and it has mediated in Congo, Chad, Liberia and Sierra Leone. And
  it has supplied most of the troops for ECOMOG, the West African force that intervened in
  Liberia in 1990 and Sierra Leone in 1997.

  The doctrine has a limit. Afrocentrism does not mean that Nigeria deals only with Africa:
  it is a member of the United Nations, the Commonwealth and OPEC, and it trades with the
  West and with Asia. Nor does it mean that Nigeria subordinates its own interest; the
  national interest remains the innermost circle, and Africa is the first circle outward.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* it defines the term, places it in the model, gives three dated examples and then guards against the standard overstatement.]

