== G.6 Question 5 — Anonymity and Impartiality

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[Distinguish the anonymity of the civil service from its impartiality.]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *one sentence each, then the difference, then one example of each being broken.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  Anonymity is the rule that a civil servant does not speak publicly for government policy:
  the minister takes the credit and the blame in public, while the official remains
  answerable inside the service and to the legislature through the accounting process.

  Impartiality is the rule that a civil servant serves whichever party is in power with
  equal loyalty, and does not use the office to favour a party or a candidate.

  The difference is the direction of the duty. Anonymity is about *public profile* — who
  speaks. Impartiality is about *political neutrality* — whom the official serves. An
  official can be perfectly anonymous and still partial, leaking a document to help a
  favoured party; and an official can be scrupulously impartial and still break anonymity by
  defending government policy on television.

  Both canons protect the same thing: the service's ability to serve a government that the
  voters may replace, without the machinery of administration becoming the property of a
  party.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* it gives both definitions, names the axis of the difference, shows how each canon can fail independently, and explains the common purpose.]

