== G.7 Question 6 — The 2022 Act

#block(width: 100%, fill: rgb("#F1F5F9"), stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 6pt, inset: 9pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-navy)[QUESTION]
  #v(2pt)
  #text(size: 9.8pt)[State four changes introduced by the Electoral Act 2022 and explain why each matters.]
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.8pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[PLAN:] *four changes, each with its effect on the cycle.*

#text(size: 9.8pt)[
  The Act introduced the Bimodal Voter Accreditation System, which verifies a voter by
  fingerprint and facial recognition. It matters because impersonation and multiple
  registration become much harder when the person, not just the card, must be verified.

  It permitted the electronic transmission of results from the polling unit, subject to
  network coverage certified by the regulator. It matters because transmission reduces the
  opportunity to alter figures between the polling unit and the collation centre.

  It required parties to choose their candidates by direct or indirect primaries conducted
  according to their own constitutions, and it limited campaign spending. It matters because
  a candidate imposed by a godfather, or one who buys the nomination, is a candidate the
  voters did not choose.

  It gave INEC the power to review a result where the declared votes exceed the number of
  accredited voters — the over-voting rule. It matters because it turns the accreditation
  data into a check on the count itself.

  Together the four changes attack the accreditation, collation and nomination stages of the
  cycle, which is where the most damaging frauds have occurred.
]

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.4pt, fill: jamb-muted)[*Why it scores:* four changes, four reasons, and a closing sentence that maps them onto the cycle rather than listing them in the air.]

